    "result_text": "The change is complete and the build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## What I implemented\n\nThe spec: *a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum among their household members; return the capped value; with no existing household member, no cap applies.*\n\n`membershipLevel` isn't stored \u2014 it's derived on every read by `MembershipLevel.of(owner)` (used by the response mapper). So the cap is computed once at create time, persisted, and applied wherever the level is derived:\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** \u2014 added a persisted nullable `membershipLevelCap` (`Integer`, column `membership_level_cap`), added to all four schema dialects (h2, hsqldb, postgres, mysql).\n\n2. **`AssignMembershipLevelCap`** (new OfficeFloor function) \u2014 scans existing owners sharing the new owner's `householdId`, takes their maximum `MembershipLevel.of(...)`, and sets the cap to `max + 1`. No household id or no existing member \u2192 leaves the cap `null` (no cap). Wired into `owners.POST.yml` right after `assignHouseholdSize` (household id already computed; runs before `save`, so the new owner isn't in the scan).\n\n3. **`MembershipLevel.of`** \u2014 now returns `min(derivedLevel, cap)` when a cap is set, unchanged otherwise. This makes both the audit log and the GET response reflect the capped value.\n\n## One necessary adjustment beyond the cap itself\n\nThe new acceptance test creates two owners in the *same* household (same lastName + postcode) and expects both to succeed. `CheckOwnerIdentityUnique` was rejecting the second with a 409 based on `householdId` alone \u2014 but its own POST.yml comment, the exception's javadoc, and `AssignPossibleDuplicate`'s javadoc all state it should reject only on a **whole `identityKey`** match (telephone | email | householdId). I aligned the code with that documented behavior, so same-household owners with different telephones now coexist (the later one is flagged a soft `possibleDuplicate`, as `AssignPossibleDuplicate` already handles) and the cap rule can apply. Exact-identity duplicates are still rejected.\n\nI confirmed the cap is exercised, not vacuous: the audit log shows the two household owners created with `membershipLevel=1` then `membershipLevel=2` (level 2 = 1 + 1, the cap in action).",
